    "Now I have the complete picture. Here's my plan:\n\n---\n\n## Newsletter System — Improvement Plan\n\n### What exists today\n\nYou already have a solid foundation:\n- A **compose UI** with 5 slots (2×2 grid + 1 bottom banner), article picker sidebar, type filtering\n- A **Bulles et Aventures** background image (`bulles-bg.jpg`) — the underwater scene with galleon, diver, fish, globe, and 5 white boxes\n- A **`_frame.blade.php`** partial that renders the visual preview using absolute positioning over the background image\n- 3 alternative gradient themes (Abyss, Coral, Arctic)\n- An **approval workflow** (3 bureau members must approve before sending)\n- A **`renderEmailHtml()`** method that generates email-safe HTML with a 2-column table layout\n- Sliced decorative images (`header.jpg`, `row1-left.jpg`, `footer.jpg`, etc.) — these are the individual pieces of the background\n\n### The problem\n\nThe current email rendering (`renderEmailHtml()`) doesn't match the visual template your colleagues designed. It sends a basic table layout with a header image and white cards on a blue background. The beautiful galleon scene, the decorative borders, and the overall \"magazine\" feel are lost in the email.\n\nThe `_frame.blade.php` preview uses CSS `position:absolute` over the full background image — this works in a browser but **will not work in email clients** (Outlook, Gmail, Yahoo all strip or break absolute positioning).\n\n### Proposed approach: Sliced table-based email template\n\nEmail HTML is stuck in 1999 — it must use `<table>` layouts, inline styles, no CSS grid/flexbox, no `position:absolute`. The way to achieve the visual richness of your template is the classic **image slicing** technique, which you've already started with the `bulles/` folder.\n\n**The layout as a 7-row table:**\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│         HEADER IMAGE            │  ← header.jpg (full width, title + month)\n├────────┬──────────┬─────────────┤\n│ row1-  │  SLOT 1  │ row1-       │  ← decorative left/right borders\n│ left   │  (card)  │ center      │\n│        ├──────────┤             │\n│        │  SLOT 2  │             │\n├────────┴──────────┴─────────────┤\n│         H-SEPARATOR             │  ← h-separator.jpg (decorative divider)\n├────────┬──────────┬─────────────┤\n│ row2-  │  SLOT 3  │ row2-       │\n│ left   │  (card)  │ center      │\n│        ├──────────┤             │\n│        │  SLOT 4  │             │\n├────────┴──────────┴─────────────┤\n│     [ SLOT 5 - banner ]         │  ← centered over footer area\n├─────────────────────────────────┤\n│         FOOTER IMAGE            │  ← footer.jpg (galleon, coral, fish)\n└─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nEach \"slot\" is a white card with article image, title, excerpt, and \"Read more\" link — rendered as a nested table inside the cell.\n\n**On mobile (< 480px):** The 2-column rows stack to single column via `@media` queries (with `display:block !important` on `<td>` elements). Gmail app doesn't support media queries, but the 300px column width is already readable on phone. For Gmail, we use the \"fluid hybrid\" technique: each column is a `<div>` with `display:inline-block; max-width:280px; width:100%` inside the `<td>`.\n\n### Monthly theme rotation\n\nInstead of one fixed background, create a **theme system** with interchangeable image sets:\n\n| Theme | Header | Borders | Footer | Mood |\n|-------|--------|---------|--------|------|\n| Bulles (current) | Underwater globe + title | Fish, bubbles | Galleon + coral reef | Adventurous |\n| Abyss | Deep ocean, bioluminescence | Dark water, jellyfish | Abyssal trench | Mysterious |\n| Tropical | Turquoise lagoon, palm trees | Colorful fish, coral | Beach + sunset | Warm |\n| Wreck | Shipwreck silhouette | Rusty metal, barnacles | Anchor + chain | Historical |\n| Arctic | Ice shelf, polar water | Ice crystals, seals | Iceberg + aurora | Cool |\n| Méditerranée | Calanques, clear blue | Posidonia, grouper | Port + fishing boats | Local |\n\nEach theme is a folder under `public/images/newsletter/` with the same file naming convention: `header.jpg`, `row1-left.jpg`, ..., `footer.jpg`. The compose UI already has a theme selector — just extend it to pick from these folders.\n\n**AI-generated images:** These decorative borders and headers can be generated with DALL-E or Midjourney at the right dimensions. I can generate the slice specifications (exact pixel sizes) so you or a designer can produce them.\n\n### Implementation steps\n\n1. **Rebuild `renderEmailHtml()` as a Blade template** (`newsletters/themes/email.blade.php`) — replace the PHP string concatenation with a proper Blade view that generates email-safe HTML tables. This makes it editable and previewable.\n\n2. **Use the sliced images as table cell backgrounds** — the decorative borders (`row1-left.jpg`, etc.) become `background` attributes on `<td>` elements (supported by all email clients via `background` attribute, not CSS).\n\n3. **Add a \"Preview as Email\" button** — renders the Blade template in an iframe so you see exactly what recipients will get, before sending.\n\n4. **Responsive stacking** — use the fluid hybrid technique (inline-block divs inside table cells) so the 2-column layout gracefully stacks on mobile without relying on media queries.\n\n5. **Theme folder structure** — each theme is a directory with 9 images. The compose UI shows a visual thumbnail of each theme. Selecting a theme just changes which image folder is referenced.\n\n6. **Unsubscribe footer** — add a one-click unsubscribe link (required by Gmail/Yahoo since Feb 2024) using `List-Unsubscribe` header and a footer link.\n\n### What I'd build (files)\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `resources/views/admin/newsletters/themes/email.blade.php` | The email-safe table-based template |\n| `resources/views/admin/newsletters/themes/preview.blade.php` | Browser preview wrapper (iframe-friendly) |\n| Update `NewsletterController::renderEmailHtml()` | Use the Blade template instead of string concatenation |\n| Update `_frame.blade.php` | Match the email layout more closely in the compose preview |\n| `public/images/newsletter/{theme}/` folders | One folder per theme with 9 standardized slices |\n\n### Image slice specifications\n\nFor the current Bulles theme (and any future theme), each set needs:\n\n| Image | Dimensions | Purpose |\n|-------|-----------|---------|\n| `header.jpg` | 600×200px | Title banner, full width |\n| `row1-left.jpg` | 40×280px | Left decorative border, rows 1-2 |\n| `row1-right.jpg` | 40×280px | Right decorative border, rows 1-2 |\n| `h-separator.jpg` | 600×20px | Horizontal divider between row pairs |\n| `row2-left.jpg` | 40×280px | Left border, rows 3-4 |\n| `row2-right.jpg` | 40×280px | Right border, rows 3-4 |\n| `footer.jpg` | 600×200px | Bottom scene (galleon, coral, etc.) |\n| `bg-tile.jpg` | 600×10px | Optional repeating background for the body |\n| `slot5-bg.jpg` | 400×50px | Background for the bottom banner slot |\n\nAll at 600px max width (email standard). Retina: provide @2x versions at 1200px.\n\n### What NOT to do\n\n- Don't send the full `bulles-bg.jpg` as a single background image with text overlaid — Outlook doesn't support CSS `background-image` on `<div>`, and Gmail strips it. The slicing approach is the only reliable way.\n- Don't use `<style>` blocks — Gmail strips them. All styles must be inline.\n- Don't use web fonts — stick to Arial/Georgia/serif stacks.\n\n---\n\nWant me to start building this? I'd begin with the email Blade template and the updated `renderEmailHtml()` method.\n[Tool uses: none]",
