    "Deployed. Here's how the translation quality system now works:\n\n**Change detection:** Every translation stores a `source_hash` (xxh3 of title+body). When an article is edited, `markStaleIfChanged()` compares the current hash — only translations whose source actually changed get marked stale. No more blanket invalidation.\n\n**Word count validation:** Source and translated word counts are tracked. If the ratio falls outside 30%–300% (e.g. a 500-word French article translates to only 50 words in German), the translation is flagged as suspicious and marked stale for re-translation.\n\n**Retry logic:** Failed API calls increment a `retries` counter instead of silently failing. After 3 failed attempts, the translation is flagged for admin attention.\n\n**Hourly scheduled task does 4 things:**\n1. Translates one new untranslated article to all locales\n2. Refreshes up to 5 stale translations (skips flagged ones, respects retry limit)\n3. Auto-flags translations that exhausted 3 retries\n4. Scans for word count anomalies and flags them\n\n**Flagged translations** have `flagged_at` + `flag_reason` — these are visible in the admin and won't be auto-retried until an admin reviews them. The `flag_reason` explains what went wrong (e.g. \"Word count ratio: 450 → 12\" or \"Failed after 3 retries\").\n[Tool uses: none]",
