    "All deployed. Here's the summary:\n\n---\n\n**Scheduled Task Heartbeat** — now on the admin dashboard at the bottom:\n\n| Task | Schedule | What it tracks |\n|------|----------|---------------|\n| `medical-reminders` | Daily 08:00 | Expiry reminders sent |\n| `weekly-backup` | Sunday 03:00 | Backup created + pruned |\n| `translations` | Hourly | New articles translated, stale refreshed, flagged |\n| `vote-auto` | Every minute | Votes auto-opened/closed |\n| `audit-purge` | Monthly 1st | Old audit logs deleted |\n| `classifieds-cleanup` | Monthly 1st | Expired ads unpublished/deleted |\n| `equipment-reminders` | Daily 09:00 | Overdue loan notifications |\n\nStatus badges: 🟢 OK, 🔴 Failed, 🟡 Overdue (>25h since last run), ⚪ Never run.\n\nThe table will populate as the cron runs each task. Within an hour you should see `vote-auto` and `translations` show up as the first heartbeats.\n\n---\n\n**Newsletter lessons** are in the report above. The key improvements for the compose UI are: editable teaser text per slot, custom URL per slot, \"Send test to myself\" button, and a bilingual toggle. Want me to implement any of those next?\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> about the lessons from the newsletter: 2 i would prefer a bottom left \"EN>>\" (or \"English>\") link in each tile while the rest goes to FR. 3, 4 yes. 1 and 5 we may need to support this for a few months only while the new site is not the main one. 6 we made a change in the article at the last minute, it is OK. let us discuss 7 and whehter SMTP is needed when we have IMAP ?",
    "\n\nGood points. Let me address each:\n\n**2 — EN link in each tile:** Makes sense — a small \"EN ›\" link at the bottom-right of each white card, linking to the English version of the same article. Keeps the layout clean, no separate EN row needed.\n\n**3 — \"Send test to myself\":** Will implement.\n\n**4 — Editable teaser per slot:** Will implement.\n\n**1 & 5 — Custom URL per slot:** Agreed, temporary need. I'll add it as an optional override field per slot — if blank, uses the normal article URL. Easy to remove later.\n\n**6 — Last-minute article edit:** Fair enough, no change needed.\n\n**7 — SMTP vs Resend API:**\n\nYou're right to question this. Here's the situation:\n\n- **IMAP** (port 993) = reading mail. You have this via Roundcube. ✅\n- **SMTP** (port 587) = sending mail from a mail client (Roundcube, Thunderbird). This works **internally** between `@ecb.pm` addresses. ✅\n- **SMTP outbound** (port 25) = delivering mail to Gmail, Yahoo, etc. This is what Hetzner blocks. ❌\n\n**Resend replaces only the outbound delivery** (port 25). You don't need SMTP relay at all if you send newsletters via Resend's API directly from the app. The flow would be:\n\n```\nNewsletter \"Send\" button → Laravel calls Resend API → Resend delivers to Gmail/Yahoo/etc.\n```\n\nNo SMTP involved. The app makes an HTTPS POST to `api.resend.com` with the HTML and recipient list. This is actually better than SMTP relay because:\n- No port 25 dependency\n- Better deliverability (Resend/SES handles reputation)\n- Delivery tracking built in\n- Works from anywhere (even Wasmer Edge)\n\nFor **transactional emails** (password reset, verification, medical cert notifications), the app currently uses `Mail::raw()` which goes through the local SMTP. Those would also need Resend, or you wait for Hetzner to unblock port 25.\n\n**My recommendation:** Configure Resend as the Laravel mail driver for everything. One line in `.env`:\n\n```\nMAIL_MAILER=resend\nRESEND_API_KEY=re_WS6sGZ3W_...\n```\n\nThis way all mail (newsletters, transactional, notifications) goes through Resend's API. No SMTP needed at all. The docker-mailserver stays for **receiving** mail (IMAP) and internal routing only.\n\nWant me to:\n1. Implement the newsletter UI improvements (EN link in tiles, test send, editable teaser, optional custom URL)?\n2. Configure Resend as the Laravel mail driver?\n\nOr both?\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> EN link I said at the bottom left of the tile, FR at the bottom right (default for the whole tile). is resend more or less likely to have mail classified as spam as hetzner or mailjet ? I also need to authorize clubcep.eu (or test.clubcep.eu?) if we go that way ?",
    "\n\n**EN bottom-left, FR bottom-right** — got it, will do.\n\n**Spam likelihood comparison:**\n\n| Provider | Reputation | Shared/Dedicated IP | Spam risk |\n|----------|-----------|-------------------|-----------|\n| **Resend** (via Amazon SES) | Excellent — Amazon's IP pools are well-maintained | Shared (free tier) | Low |\n| **Hetzner direct** | Unknown — fresh IP, no sending history | Your own IP | Medium-high initially (needs warmup) |\n| **Mailjet** | Good — established sender | Shared | Low |\n\nResend is on par with Mailjet for deliverability. Both use major infrastructure (Amazon SES / Mailgun) behind the scenes. Hetzner direct would be worse initially because your IP has no sending reputation.\n\n**Domain verification for Resend:**\n\nYou verified `ecb.pm` already — that's what we used to send `newsletter@ecb.pm`. If you want to send as `noreply@clubcep.eu` or `newsletter@clubcep.eu`, you'd need to verify `clubcep.eu` in Resend too (same process — 3 DNS records).\n\nFor now, sending from `newsletter@ecb.pm` works fine. You can verify `clubcep.eu` later when you're ready to switch the \"From\" address.\n\n**For `test.clubcep.eu`** — no need to verify subdomains separately. If you verify `clubcep.eu`, you can send from any `@clubcep.eu` address. Subdomains like `test.clubcep.eu` would need their own verification only if you want to send from `@test.clubcep.eu`.\n\nWant me to go ahead and implement the newsletter UI improvements + Resend as Laravel mail driver?\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> go for UI improvements, not mail yet. Is there no reverse lookup involved when using resend that would tell them the machine is behind test.clubcep.eu and not clubcep.eu ?",
    "\n\nNo reverse lookup issue. When you use Resend's API, the email is sent from **Resend's servers** (Amazon SES infrastructure), not from your server. The receiving mail server (Gmail) sees:\n\n- **SMTP connection from:** Amazon SES IP (e.g. `54.240.x.x`) — excellent reputation\n- **Reverse DNS of that IP:** `amazonses.com` — trusted\n- **DKIM signature:** signed with the key you added to `ecb.pm` DNS\n- **SPF check:** passes because `send.ecb.pm` includes `amazonses.com`\n- **From header:** `newsletter@ecb.pm`\n\nYour server at `test.clubcep.eu` / `178.104.123.117` is never involved in the SMTP transaction. It just makes an HTTPS API call to Resend. Gmail never sees your server's IP or hostname.\n\nNow let me implement the newsletter UI improvements:\n[Tool uses: fs_read]",
